  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole gave them a starter's line and they still couldn't finish it.",
    "what_this_means": "Gerrit Cole threw seven innings, allowed four runs on six hits, and struck out three. That is a start built for a win. Instead, the Yankees left eight runners on base across critical innings. Two in the second when the game was young, three more in the fifth when they could have answered back. And took a 3-4 loss to San Diego. Cody Bellinger sparked a three-run eighth-inning rally that pulled them within one, but it arrived too late to matter. John Schreiber held the line in the ninth, but the damage was already done. Nine strikeouts and seven hits is not an offense ready to win games where the starter delivers. This team doesn't get to leave runners on in the second and fifth and expect to survive against anyone.",
    "what_to_watch": "Colorado's Gabriel Hughes carries a 6.19 ERA into tomorrow night. The Yankees get another chance to prove that yesterday's strikeout total was an outlier, not the baseline.",
